  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents powered by large language models has revolutionized software development, enabling automated code generation, debugging, and execution. However, these powerful agents introduce significant security concerns, including prompt injection, code execution vulnerabilities, and adversarial manipulation. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine the architecture and capabilities of modern code agents, analyze security vulnerabilities introduced by agent-based code execution, and review state-of-the-art red-teaming techniques for evaluating agent safety. We discuss the emergence of automated red-teaming frameworks that systematically probe code agents for vulnerabilities, and highlight the critical importance of robust security evaluation in deploying code agents in production environments. Our analysis reveals that while code agents offer substantial productivity benefits, their security posture requires significant improvement through systematic red-teaming and robust defense mechanisms.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in software engineering, leveraging large language models (LLMs) to autonomously generate, modify, and execute code. Recent advances in LLMs have enabled code agents to perform complex programming tasks, from simple script generation to full-stack application development. These agents typically combine code generation capabilities with code execution environments, allowing them to iteratively refine their solutions based on execution feedback.\n\nThe integration of code execution capabilities introduces unique security challenges. Unlike traditional LLM applications that primarily generate text, code agents can execute arbitrary code on the user's system, creating attack surfaces for:\n\n1. **Prompt Injection Attacks**: Adversarial inputs that manipulate agent behavior through crafted prompts\n2. **Code Execution Vulnerabilities**: Exploitation of sandbox escapes and improper isolation\n3. **Supply Chain Attacks**: Malicious code injections through dependencies or external tools\n4. **Data Exfiltration**: Unauthorized access to sensitive information through code execution\n\nThe security implications are particularly concerning given the widespread adoption of code agents in production environments. Recent incidents have demonstrated that even minor vulnerabilities in code agents can lead to severe security breaches, including system compromise and data theft.\n\nTo address these challenges, the research community has begun developing systematic approaches to code agent security. Red-teaming, the practice of simulating adversarial attacks to identify vulnerabilities, has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating code agent safety. Automated red-teaming frameworks enable efficient and comprehensive security testing, going beyond manual penetration testing to systematically explore the vulnerability landscape.\n\nThis paper reviews the current state of code agent research and red-teaming methodologies. We organize our discussion as follows: Section 2 reviews related work in code agents and red-teaming. Section 3 discusses the architecture and capabilities of modern code agents. Section 4 presents current red-teaming approaches and methodologies. Section 5 examines challenges and future directions. We conclude in Section 6 with a summary of key findings and implications for future research.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Code Agent Architectures\n\nThe development of code agents has evolved from simple code completion systems to sophisticated multi-step reasoning frameworks. Early code agents relied on rule-based systems and limited context understanding [1]. More recent approaches leverage large language models with enhanced code-specific training, enabling agents to understand complex codebases and generate high-quality code.\n\nTransformer-based architectures have been fundamental to the advancement of code agents. The self-attention mechanism introduced in [2] has become the backbone of most modern code generation models, enabling effective modeling of long-range dependencies in code. Recent works have extended these architectures with specialized modules for code reasoning, execution planning, and context management.\n\nSeveral notable code agent frameworks have emerged in recent years. AutoCodeRover [3] combines code navigation with LLM-based generation to autonomously fix bugs in real-world codebases. AgentCoder [4] integrates multiple agent collaboration for complex software development tasks. CodeAct [5] enables agents to interact with external tools and APIs through code execution. These systems demonstrate the growing sophistication of code agents and their potential for automating complex software engineering workflows.\n\n### 2.2 Red-teaming Methodologies\n\nRed-teaming has become an essential practice for evaluating AI system safety. Traditional red-teaming involves human experts attempting to break into systems or identify vulnerabilities through creative attack strategies [6]. Automated red-teaming extends this approach by developing algorithms that systematically explore attack spaces and identify vulnerabilities.\n\nRecent work has adapted red-teaming approaches specifically for LLM applications. GCG (Greedy Coordinate Gradient) [7] generates adversarial prompts through gradient-based optimization. AutoDAN [8] uses evolutionary algorithms to create natural-sounding adversarial prompts. These methods have been successfully applied to jailbreak defenses and safety evaluation.\n\nThe application of red-teaming to code agents is an emerging research area. Recent work by Guo et al. [9] introduced RedCodeAgent, an automated red-teaming framework specifically designed for code agents. This framework systematically tests diverse code agents against various attack vectors, including prompt injection, code execution manipulation, and sandbox escape attempts. RedCodeAgent employs a multi-stage approach, combining automated attack generation with dynamic vulnerability assessment, and has demonstrated the ability to discover previously unknown vulnerabilities in state-of-the-art code agents.\n\n### 2.3 Security of Code Execution Environments\n\nSecuring code execution environments is critical for code agent safety. Early approaches relied on simple sandboxing techniques, which proved insufficient against sophisticated attacks [10]. Modern approaches combine multiple security mechanisms, including container isolation, privilege restriction, network filtering, and behavior monitoring.\n\nRecent advances in container security have improved the safety of code execution environments. Techniques such as seccomp filtering, AppArmor profiles, and namespace isolation provide defense-in-depth protection. However, researchers continue to discover new escape vectors, particularly in complex execution environments with multiple integration points.\n\nThe research by [2] on attention mechanisms has also influenced security approaches, with attention-based models being used to detect anomalous code patterns and potential security issues in agent-generated code.\n\n## 3. Code Agent Capabilities and Vulnerabilities\n\n### 3.1 Architecture Components\n\nModern code agents typically consist of the following components:\n\n1. **Code Generation Module**: Generates code based on natural language instructions\n2. **Execution Environment**: Provides isolated code execution capabilities\n3. **Tool Integration**: Interfaces with external systems and APIs\n4. **Context Management**: Maintains state and history across interactions\n5. **Error Recovery**: Handles execution failures and iterates on solutions\n\n### 3.2 Attack Vectors\n\nCode agents face multiple attack vectors that require systematic red-teaming:\n\n- **Input Manipulation**: Adversarial prompts that cause the agent to generate malicious code\n- **Context Poisoning**: Injecting malicious content into codebases or files the agent processes\n- **Tool Exploitation**: Manipulating tool responses or exploiting tool vulnerabilities\n- **Execution Manipulation**: Crafting code that exploits the execution environment\n\n### 3.3 Red-teaming Challenges\n\nEffective red-teaming of code agents presents unique challenges:\n\n1. **Dynamic Attack Spaces**: Agents can modify their behavior based on execution feedback\n2. **Multi-step Attacks**: Attacks may require coordinating multiple interactions\n3. **Environment Complexity**: Execution environments vary widely across deployments\n4. **Evaluation Metrics**: Defining comprehensive metrics for security evaluation remains difficult\n\n## 4. Automated Red-teaming Frameworks\n\n### 4.1 Framework Design Principles\n\nModern red-teaming frameworks for code agents follow several design principles:\n\n- **Automation**: Minimize human intervention in attack generation\n- **Comprehensiveness**: Cover diverse attack categories and edge cases\n- **Adaptivity**: Adjust attack strategies based on agent behavior\n- **Assessment**: Provide detailed vulnerability reporting and severity scoring\n\n### 4.2 RedCodeAgent Approach\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework [9] implements a comprehensive red-teaming methodology:\n\n1. **Attack Generation**: Automatically generates diverse attack prompts targeting code agents\n2. **Vulnerability Testing**: Executes attacks against target agents in controlled environments\n3. **Response Analysis**: Analyzes agent responses for signs of vulnerability exploitation\n4. **Reporting**: Generates detailed reports with vulnerability descriptions and remediation suggestions\n\nRedCodeAgent has demonstrated effectiveness in discovering vulnerabilities in multiple code agent systems, including prompt injection flaws and code execution bypasses. The frame